CAT = "iespExcav"; SEG = "EXC"
# Unit 6 of Safe Pass: risk assessment for excavations. Construction Regulations 2013 Part 6 and the
# HSA excavation FAQ (inspection daily, at the start of each shift if over 2 m, after events, every
# 7 days if nobody works there; AF3 report; risk may need support even under 1.25 m).
Q = [
('How often must an excavation be inspected while people are working in it?',
 'At least once every day', ['At least once a week', 'At least once a month', 'Only when it is first dug'],
 'A competent person must inspect excavations at least once in every day during which people work there, and also after events that may affect their stability.'),
('An excavation is more than 2 metres deep. When must it be inspected?',
 'At the start of each shift', ['At the end of each week', 'Only after heavy rain', 'Once before the job starts'],
 'The HSA says excavations over 2 m deep must be inspected by a competent person at the start of each shift, before work begins.'),
('An excavation stands unused over a holiday. How often must it still be inspected?',
 'At least every 7 days', ['At least every 14 days', 'At least every 30 days', 'Not at all until it is reused'],
 'Excavations must be inspected at least once every 7 days where nobody is at work there, and again before anyone goes back in.'),
('On which form does the competent person record an excavation inspection?',
 'The AF3 form', ['The Safe Pass card', 'The accident book', 'The planning notice'],
 'The HSA says the competent person who inspects an excavation fills in an AF3 report on the day of the inspection.'),
('Can a trench less than 1.25 m deep still need its sides supported?',
 'Yes, if the risk assessment shows it', ['No, never below 1.25 m deep', 'Only in wet winter weather', 'Only if an HSA inspector asks for it'],
 'The HSA notes that poor ground or work where people lie or crouch in the trench can make support necessary even in shallow excavations.'),
('What is battering an excavation?',
 'Sloping the sides back to a safe angle', ['Driving sheet piles in with a heavy hammer', 'Filling the trench with gravel', 'Pumping water out of the trench'],
 'Battering cuts the sides to a slope at which the soil will not slip. Shoring or a trench box supports vertical sides instead.'),
('A digger operator is about to tip spoil right at the lip of a trench where two workers are laying pipe. Why is this dangerous?',
 'The extra load can collapse the trench side', ['The spoil will dry out too fast', 'It blocks the AF3 report', 'It makes the trench deeper'],
 'Spoil near the edge adds weight to the sides and can fall in. Keep spoil and materials well back from the edge.'),
('A loaded tracked excavator must work beside an open trench. What should the risk assessment consider first?',
 'Whether the sides can bear the extra load', ['Whether the operator has a radio', 'Whether the trench is in the shade', 'Whether the spoil heap is tidy'],
 "Heavy plant near the edge overloads the side wall and can collapse it. The machine's position, the ground and the trench support must be assessed, with barriers or stop blocks to keep it back."),
('What should surround an excavation that people could fall into?',
 'Barriers or edge protection', ['Coloured tape at ankle height', 'Traffic cones every 20 metres', 'Nothing during daylight'],
 'Edge protection or barriers stop people and vehicles falling in. Tape alone does not prevent a fall.'),
('A trench is too deep to step out of. What should be provided for getting in and out?',
 'A ladder tied at the top', ['The shoring frames to climb', 'A rope hanging from the edge', 'The excavator bucket to ride in'],
 'The Construction Regulations require a safe means of access and egress. A ladder secured at the top and reaching above the edge, or proper steps, gives a safe route.'),
('What must be inspected after a fall of earth into an excavation?',
 'The excavation and its support', ['Only the heap of fallen spoil', 'Only the excavator that dug it', 'Nothing, if nobody was hurt'],
 'Any accidental fall of rock or earth, or any event likely to affect stability, triggers a fresh inspection by a competent person before work continues.'),
('What can collect at the bottom of a deep excavation and make breathing dangerous?',
 'Gases such as exhaust fumes', ['Fresh air drawn down from the surface', 'Rainwater vapour and nothing else', 'Dust blown in from the spoil heap'],
 'Heavier gases and fumes from engines, generators or the ground can build up in excavations. Keep engines away and test the air where needed.'),
('A generator is needed for a pump in a trench. Where should it be placed?',
 'Outside, well away from the trench', ['At the bottom of the trench near the pump', 'On the edge directly above the workers', 'Inside the trench box for shelter'],
 'Engine exhaust contains carbon monoxide, which can build up in an excavation. Run generators outside and downwind.'),
('Which nearby structure can be undermined by digging an excavation?',
 'The foundations of a nearby building', ['The radio on a tower crane', 'The roof of the site canteen', 'The tyres of parked vans'],
 'Digging below or near foundations can remove their support. The risk must be assessed and the structure supported before work starts.'),
('What is the main danger when water seeps into an excavation?',
 'The sides can weaken and collapse', ['The trench becomes far too cold', 'The spoil turns into fine dust', 'The shoring rusts away overnight'],
 'Water softens the ground and can wash soil from behind supports. It must be pumped out safely and the sides inspected.'),
('Why should nobody work ahead of the trench support as a trench is dug?',
 'Unsupported sides can collapse suddenly', ['It slows the excavator down far too much', 'It breaks the shoring frames', 'It stops the spoil being moved away'],
 'A cubic metre of soil weighs over a tonne. Workers must stay within the protected area of the shoring or trench box.'),
('Who should be contacted before digging near gas mains?',
 'Gas Networks Ireland', ['ESB Networks only', 'The county library', 'The Road Safety Authority'],
 "Get the gas network's drawings and advice before digging. Gas mains can be damaged by excavators and cause fires or explosions."),
('What should a worker do on seeing cracks open along the edge of a trench?',
 'Get everyone out and report it', ['Fill the cracks with loose spoil', 'Keep working but work faster', 'Wait to see if the cracks grow'],
 'Tension cracks are a sign the side may collapse. Get people out first, then have a competent person inspect it.'),
("Which of these shows that a trench's shoring is not safe?",
 'Loose wedges and bent struts', ['Struts set level and horizontal', 'Wedges driven tight in place', 'Sheets standing straight and true'],
 'HSA checks include tight wedges, horizontal struts and no excessive deflection. Loose or bent parts mean the support may fail.'),
('Why should materials not be thrown into an excavation where people are working?',
 'They can strike the workers below', ['They spoil the AF3 inspection report', 'They change the type of soil', 'They make the trench deeper'],
 'Materials should be lowered in a controlled way. The Construction Regulations require precautions against falling materials in excavations.'),
]
